d III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482361 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482361 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f7a0c571-503d-5fe4-884d-519e3a27a77e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482435 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482435 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482435 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482435 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482435 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482435 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482435 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482435 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482435 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6e63f712-8c7c-524e-90a0-bc57875864f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482470 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482470 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482470 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482470 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482470 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482470 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482470 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482470 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186482314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482470 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "af4d3735-879f-5545-9728-93257a5ef240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482584 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482584 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482584 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482584 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ and $y=3.$ If $R=[-1,1]\\times [1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482584 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b79622ff-7b5d-5073-9074-7493234a4c4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482584 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/1c/1ca0b430b798a6dc85fd2b731a93690f79bb2c45b33bbe6f31a5618a2ce0888a.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482597 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482597 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482597 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482597 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f5/f5f6dae762414eb327b9eae9e5445ef1916e9efc0e17c1316339bbeb416ec94e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y-x, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ y-x, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y-x, & (x,y)\\in R∖D, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482597 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d267d682-18e1-5263-9cb3-12f52098c9f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482597 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and the rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/97/976dc1b0a7298c5cf0a331acb00594c8e4c74a14b6df3fc18f7e7997db2956c4.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $y-x$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,0)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function II equals $y-x$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482625 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "186a0fbc-5b23-5be4-8814-746a9a9a5c7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482625 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186482597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482626 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186482634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482626 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b5af6dc-d98b-5c64-b233-11f3ee5e3995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482626 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186482584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482631 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186482625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482631 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3cccf520-8b64-5626-8769-b89d045f3205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482631 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186425929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482634 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186482631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482634 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62090604-96b8-5d41-8a4a-a6c72e1082c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482634 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186348413 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
